{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget","record":{"id":"scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget","kind":"claim","label":"Integral and pointwise bounds for a minimizer","status":"unverified","source_id":"authored_long_comparisons_20261024","locator":"Long reasoning record #coefficient-budget","statement":"For an admissible pure zero-field SK minimizer and its diffusion, m(t)=E[Phi_xx(t,X_t)]=1-integral_0^t gamma, integral_0^1 gamma<=1 and 0<=gamma(t)<=m(t)/(1-t)<=1/(1-t). Also I(T)=T-integral_0^T(T-s)gamma(s)ds and P_star=1-integral_0^1(1-s)gamma(s)ds. These budget inequalities alone give neither equality integral gamma=1 nor effective mesh constants; a separate endpoint argument supplies the equality.","assumption_ids":["scalar.paper_minimizer"],"citation_ids":["authored_r8_scalar_minimizer_coefficient_budget"],"evidence_ids":["ordinary_deductions_review_20261026","ordinary_deductions_review_20261026_r9"],"boundary":"Ordinary authored deduction checked against retained mathematical inputs; no new Lean declaration, elaborated dependency, cross-model transport, Comparator acceptance or independent proof certification is asserted."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/coefficient-budget","anchor":"coefficient-budget","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/coefficient-budget","anchor":"coefficient-budget","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"coefficient-budget\"></a>\n\n**A coefficient budget from the first curvature moment.** Full support\nalso bounds the minimizing coefficient. Put $m(t)=\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)$.\nThe compact-strip identity\n$$\nda(t,X_t)=-\\gamma(t)a(t,X_t)^2\\,dt+b(t,X_t)\\,dB_t\n$$\nholds before any smoothness conclusion for $\\gamma$; the bounded strip\nderivatives make its stochastic integral a martingale.\n[OpenAI (2026a), proof of Corollary 6.4][fs-density].\nAt zero, $X_0=0$, $a(0,0)\\ge0$ and $\\mathbb E a(0,X_0)^2=1$\ngive $a(0,0)=1$. Taking expectations and using\n$\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$ therefore yields\n$$\nm(t)=1-\\int_0^t\\gamma(s)\\,ds,\\qquad 0\\le t<1.\n$$\nConvexity gives $m(t)\\ge0$. Letting $t\\uparrow1$ and using\nmonotonicity of $\\gamma$ on every tail gives\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\gamma(s)\\,ds\\le1,\\qquad\n0\\le\\gamma(t)\\le\\frac{m(t)}{1-t}\\le\\frac1{1-t}.\n$$\nIndeed, $(s-t)\\gamma(t)\\le\\int_t^s\\gamma\\le m(t)$ for\n$t<s<1$. Integrating the first-moment identity also gives\n$$\nI(T)=T-\\int_0^T(T-s)\\gamma(s)\\,ds,\\qquad\nP_*=1-\\int_0^1(1-s)\\gamma(s)\\,ds.\n$$\nThe second equality uses (6.1), so it retains that proposition's\nground-state identification. These are ordinary consequences in the\nnormalization $\\xi''=1$. The budget alone does not identify the terminal\nfirst moment. The following endpoint argument supplies that additional\ninformation.\n\n<a id=\"coefficient-total-mass\"></a>\n\n**Proposition 6.2 (terminal normalization in zero field).** For the\nminimizer and diffusion of Theorem 5.1,\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\uparrow1}m(t)=0,\\qquad\n\\int_0^1\\gamma(s)\\,ds=1,\\qquad\nm(t)=\\int_t^1\\gamma(s)\\,ds,\n$$\nand consequently\n$$\nP_*=\\int_0^1s\\gamma(s)\\,ds.\n$$\nThus $\\gamma(s)\\,ds$ has total mass one; this does not normalize the\nStieltjes measure $d\\gamma$. This is an ordinary analytic consequence,\nseparate from the selected formal endpoints.\n\n**Proof.** The backward comparison in §4.3 gives\n$\\Phi_{xxx}(t,x)\\le0$ for $x>0$ in every positive finite-step\napproximation. Compact-strip convergence of the third spatial derivative\npasses this inequality to $\\gamma$. Together with evenness and convexity,\nit says that $a(t,x)$ is nonnegative, even and nonincreasing in $|x|$.\nThese inputs are [OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 3.2][fs-signs] and\n[Lemma 2.1][fs-reg]; they do not require differentiability of $\\gamma$.\nUniform terminal convergence to $|x|$ and convex difference quotients\ngive $u(t,x)\\to1$ at every fixed $x>0$. For each $\\delta>0$,\n$$\n0\\le a(t,\\delta)\n\\le\\frac2\\delta\\int_{\\delta/2}^{\\delta}a(t,x)\\,dx\n=\\frac2\\delta\\bigl(u(t,\\delta)-u(t,\\delta/2)\\bigr)\n\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nThe spatial monotonicity is essential here: convergence of convex\nfunctions alone would not imply convergence of their second derivatives.\n\nIt remains to control the probability of the shrinking region where\ncurvature can be large. Oddness and monotonicity give $u(t,x)\\ge0$\nfor $x\\ge0$, so the drift points away from zero. Tanaka's formula for\n$R_t=|X_t|$ gives\n$$\nR_t=W_t+V_t+L_t,\\qquad\nW_t=\\int_0^t\\operatorname{sgn}(X_s)\\,dB_s,\\qquad\nV_t=\\int_0^t\\gamma(s)u(s,R_s)\\,ds.\n$$\nHere $L$ is the local time of $X$ at zero.\nThe occupation-time formula and $d\\langle X\\rangle_s=ds$ imply\n$\\int_0^t\\mathbf1_{\\{X_s=0\\}}\\,ds=0$. Thus $\\langle W\\rangle_t=t$,\nand the continuous martingale $W$ is Brownian motion. The process $V$\nis nondecreasing and starts at zero; $L$ increases only when $R=0$.\nThe reflection formula therefore gives, pathwise,\n$$\nR_t=W_t+V_t-\\min\\left(0,\\inf_{s\\le t}(W_s+V_s)\\right)\n\\ge W_t-\\min\\left(0,\\inf_{s\\le t}W_s\\right)=:R_t^0.\n$$\nIndeed $0\\le V_s\\le V_t$ bounds the subtracted minimum by\n$\\min(0,\\inf_{s\\le t}W_s)+V_t$. No independence of $V$ and $W$\nis needed. Reflected Brownian motion $R_t^0$ has the one-time law\n$|B_t|$, so for $t>0$,\n$$\n\\Pr(|X_t|\\le\\delta)\n\\le\\Pr(|B_t|\\le\\delta)\n\\le\\sqrt{\\frac2\\pi}\\frac{\\delta}{\\sqrt t}.\n$$\nSplitting at $|X_t|=\\delta$, using spatial monotonicity outside and\nCauchy–Schwarz with $\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)^2=1$ inside, yields\n$$\n0\\le m(t)\\le a(t,\\delta)\n              +\\sqrt{\\Pr(|X_t|\\le\\delta)}.\n$$\nFirst let $t\\uparrow1$ and then $\\delta\\downarrow0$. This proves\n$m(1-)=0$. The compact-strip identity for $m$ now gives total mass one\nand the tail formula. Tonelli and (6.1) give the stated expression for\n$P_*$. In particular,\n$$\n0\\le P_*-I(T)=\\int_T^1(s-T)\\gamma(s)\\,ds\n\\le(1-T)m(T)=o(1-T).\n$$\nNo numerical rate for $m(T)\\to0$ or for the required spatial derivative\nbounds follows from this proof.\n\nThe endpoint distinction also matters when comparing editions.\n[Sellke, arXiv:2105.03506v4, Lemma 2.9, equation (2.7)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.03506v4)\nstates the expected-Hessian tail equality. In\n[version 6, Lemma 2.9, equation (2.8), and Remark 2.10](https://arxiv.org/html/2105.03506v6#S2.SS3),\nthe general statement is an inequality; the remark obtains equality\nindirectly for even mixtures. The specialized zero-field proof above\nsupplies its own endpoint argument from the shape and contact identities,\nrather than substituting $t=1$ into a compact-strip formula or relying on\nthe older equality in its full stated generality.\n","span":{"start_byte":86004,"end_byte":91025,"start_line":1923,"end_line":2047},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=coefficient-budget","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=coefficient-budget"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:f9bc54b660aede2c1f6decbbafa3e87bdb7482f03cb9d73c0f77b4d43d8e1981","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.paper_minimizer","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget%22%2C%22scalar.minimizer_coefficient_budget%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45d